('node-3-5', 'phase-03-lesson-05-two-pointers-opposing-direction-convergin', 'module-5', 5, 'Lesson 5.5: Two Pointers: Opposing Direction Converging', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 3.2', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 3.2 | Subtopics: 4 items', 'Systems project application', 160, 4, 870.0, 250.0, '# Lesson 3.5: Two Pointers: Opposing Direction Converging

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Lesson 3.2
- **Subtopics**:
  - `3.5.1` The two-pointer technique: using two coordinate indices simultaneously to inspect an array.
  - `3.5.2` Opposing pointers: starting Left at index 0 and Right at index N-1, stepping inward toward each other.
  - `3.5.3` Solving Two-Sum on sorted arrays: moving Left when sum is too small, moving Right when sum is too large.
  - `3.5.4` Why two pointers turns an O(N^2) brute-force nested search into an optimal single-pass O(N) solution.
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Pointer crossing bugs: forgetting the while left < right boundary condition, causing pointers to cross.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Implement two-pointer Two-Sum on a sorted list and prove it finds target pairs in single-pass O(N) time.
- **Project Application**: StreamBuffer: Paired token matching.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 3 // Lesson 3.5: Two Pointers: Opposing Direction Converging\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Implement two-pointer Two-Sum on a sorted list and prove it finds target pairs in single-pass O(N) t\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "3.5", "subtopics_count": 4, "verification_criteria": "Implement two-pointer Two-Sum on a sorted list and prove it finds target pairs in single-pass O(N) time.", "subtopics": ["3.5.1 The two-pointer technique: using two coordinate indices simultaneously to inspect an array.", "3.5.2 Opposing pointers: starting Left at index 0 and Right at index N-1, stepping inward toward each other.", "3.5.3 Solving Two-Sum on sorted arrays: moving Left when sum is too small, moving Right when sum is too large.", "3.5.4 Why two pointers turns an O(N^2) brute-force nested search into an optimal single-pass O(N) solution."]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Pointer crossing bugs: forgetting the while left < right boundary condition, causing pointers to cross.", "How does Two Pointers: Opposing Direction Converging scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-3-6', 'phase-03-lesson-06-two-pointers-fast-slow-pointer-cycle-dete', 'module-5', 6, 'Lesson 5.6: Two Pointers: Fast & Slow Pointer (Cycle Detection)', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 3.5', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 3.5 | Subtopics: 4 items', 'Systems project application', 160, 4, 750.0, 300.0, '# Lesson 3.6: Two Pointers: Fast & Slow Pointer (Cycle Detection)

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Lesson 3.5
- **Subtopics**:
  - `3.6.1` Same-direction pointers: two pointers starting at the beginning and moving forward at different speeds.
  - `3.6.2` The Tortoise and Hare algorithm: slow pointer moves 1 step while fast pointer moves 2 steps.
  - `3.6.3` Cycle detection intuition: if a runner and a walker circle a closed track, the runner will eventually lap the walker.
  - `3.6.4` Applications: detecting infinite loops, finding list midpoints, and cycle detection in state graphs.
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Dereferencing null pointer in fast.next.next without checking if fast or fast.next is None.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Implement Floyd''s cycle-finding algorithm to detect whether a linked sequence contains an infinite loop.
- **Project Application**: StreamBuffer: Agent loop cycle detection.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 3 // Lesson 3.6: Two Pointers: Fast & Slow Pointer (Cycle Detection)\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Implement Floyd''s cycle-finding algorithm to detect whether a linked sequence contains an infinite l\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "3.6", "subtopics_count": 4, "verification_criteria": "Implement Floyd''s cycle-finding algorithm to detect whether a linked sequence contains an infinite loop.", "subtopics": ["3.6.1 Same-direction pointers: two pointers starting at the beginning and moving forward at different speeds.", "3.6.2 The Tortoise and Hare algorithm: slow pointer moves 1 step while fast pointer moves 2 steps.", "3.6.3 Cycle detection intuition: if a runner and a walker circle a closed track, the runner will eventually lap the walker.", "3.6.4 Applications: detecting infinite loops, finding list midpoints, and cycle detection in state graphs."]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Dereferencing null pointer in fast.next.next without checking if fast or fast.next is None.", "How does Two Pointers: Fast & Slow Pointer (Cycle Detection) scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-3-7', 'phase-03-lesson-07-sliding-window-fixed-size-subarrays', 'module-5', 7, 'Lesson 5.7: Sliding Window: Fixed Size Subarrays', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 3.2', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 3.2 | Subtopics: 4 items', 'Systems project application', 160, 4, 810.0, 350.0, '# Lesson 3.7: Sliding Window: Fixed Size Subarrays

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Lesson 3.2
- **Subtopics**:
  - `3.7.1` The sliding window paradigm: maintaining a visible slice of size K across a continuous stream of data.
  - `3.7.2` Incremental updates: sliding the window by adding the new element on the right and subtracting the old on left.
  - `3.7.3` Why sliding windows achieve O(N): avoiding re-calculating the entire window from scratch on every step.
  - `3.7.4` Practical applications: calculating rolling average CPU usage, moving token limits, and network throughput.
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Recomputing sum(window) inside every step, degenerating the algorithm back to O(N * K).
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Write a function that calculates the maximum sum of any contiguous subarray of fixed length K in O(N) time.
- **Project Application**: StreamBuffer: Rolling metrics sliding window.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 3 // Lesson 3.7: Sliding Window: Fixed Size Subarrays\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Write a function that calculates the maximum sum of any contiguous subarray of fixed length K in O(N\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "3.7", "subtopics_count": 4, "verification_criteria": "Write a function that calculates the maximum sum of any contiguous subarray of fixed length K in O(N) time.", "subtopics": ["3.7.1 The sliding window paradigm: maintaining a visible slice of size K across a continuous stream of data.", "3.7.2 Incremental updates: sliding the window by adding the new element on the right and subtracting the old on left.", "3.7.3 Why sliding windows achieve O(N): avoiding re-calculating the entire window from scratch on every step.", "3.7.4 Practical applications: calculating rolling average CPU usage, moving token limits, and network throughput."]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Recomputing sum(window) inside every step, degenerating the algorithm back to O(N * K).", "How does Sliding Window: Fixed Size Subarrays scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-3-8', 'phase-03-lesson-08-sliding-window-dynamic-size-subarrays', 'module-5', 8, 'Lesson 5.8: Sliding Window: Dynamic Size Subarrays', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 3.7', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 3.7 | Subtopics: 4 items', 'Systems project application', 160, 4, 870.0, 400.0, '# Lesson 3.8: Sliding Window: Dynamic Size Subarrays

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Lesson 3.7
- **Subtopics**:
  - `3.8.1` Flexible windows: expanding the right edge until a condition is met, then contracting the left edge.
  - `3.8.2` The dynamic pattern: expand right to explore, shrink left to satisfy constraints.
  - `3.8.3` Tracking window state: keeping character frequency counts or running sums inside the window.
  - `3.8.4` Canonical problems: shortest subarray with sum >= target, longest substring without repeating characters.
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Shrinking the left pointer past the right pointer or failing to update the window state dictionary.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Find the length of the longest substring without repeating characters using a dynamic sliding window in O(N).
- **Project Application**: StreamBuffer: Variable-length context buffer slicing.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 3 // Lesson 3.8: Sliding Window: Dynamic Size Subarrays\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Find the length of the longest substring without repeating characters using a dynamic sliding window\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "3.8", "subtopics_count": 4, "verification_criteria": "Find the length of the longest substring without repeating characters using a dynamic sliding window in O(N).", "subtopics": ["3.8.1 Flexible windows: expanding the right edge until a condition is met, then contracting the left edge.", "3.8.2 The dynamic pattern: expand right to explore, shrink left to satisfy constraints.", "3.8.3 Tracking window state: keeping character frequency counts or running sums inside the window.", "3.8.4 Canonical problems: shortest subarray with sum >= target, longest substring without repeating characters."]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Shrinking the left pointer past the right pointer or failing to update the window state dictionary.", "How does Sliding Window: Dynamic Size Subarrays scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-3-9', 'phase-03-lesson-09-prefix-sums-range-sum-query-in-o-1', 'module-5', 9, 'Lesson 5.9: Prefix Sums: Range Sum Query in O(1)', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 3.2', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 3.2 | Subtopics: 4 items', 'Systems project application', 160, 4, 750.0, 450.0, '# Lesson 3.9: Prefix Sums: Range Sum Query in O(1)

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Lesson 3.2
- **Subtopics**:
  - `3.9.1` Pre-computing cumulative sums: building an array where prefix[i] stores the sum of all elements up to i.
  - `3.9.2` Instant range queries: calculating the sum between index L and R in exact O(1) time: prefix[R] - prefix[L-1].
  - `3.9.3` Trading memory for speed: spending O(N) storage to make all future range calculations instantaneous.
  - `3.9.4` Applications: computing cumulative cost over time windows and fast 2D image box filtering.
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Off-by-one errors when querying ranges that start at index 0, requiring a padded 0 at prefix[0].
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Build a PrefixSum class that takes an array of numbers and answers 1,000 range sum queries in O(1) each.
